# Oscillations 31 — Frequency shift of an anharmonic oscillator (Landau–Lifshitz §28)
#
# (Landau & Lifshitz, Mechanics, §28, eq. 28.13; Goldstein 3rd ed. §11.6 (Lindstedt);
#  José & Saletan §7.3.)
# A particle moves in the potential U(x)/m = ½ ω₀² x² + ⅓ α x³ + ¼ β x⁴, so
#     x'' = −ω₀² x − α x² − β x³,
# with ω₀ = 2.00 rad/s, α = 0.300 m⁻¹ s⁻², β = 0.500 m⁻² s⁻². Landau's second-order
# perturbation theory gives, for oscillation amplitude a,
#     ω = ω₀ + (3β/(8ω₀) − 5α²/(12ω₀³)) a²  +  O(a³).
#   (a) For releases from rest at x₀ = 0.05, 0.10, 0.20 and 0.40 m, find the period
#       from the equation of motion: the first time x' = 0 again is half a period.
#   (b) The same period exactly, from energy conservation:
#       T = 2 ∫_{x₋}^{x₀} dx / √(2 (U(x₀) − U(x))/m), with x₋ < 0 the other turning point.
#   (c) Compare the measured shift coefficient (ω − ω₀)/x₀² with Landau's
#       coefficient; the difference should shrink roughly in proportion to x₀.

ω0 = 2.00 rad/s
α = 0.300 [1/(m s²)]    # `0.300 /(m s²)` is refused ("m isn't defined")
β = 0.500 [1/(m² s²)]
U(x) = ½ ω0² x² + α x³ / 3 + β x⁴ / 4
k_LL = 3 β / (8 ω0) - 5 α² / (12 ω0³)
print "Landau coefficient =", k_LL to 8 digits

half_period(x0) =
    solve x'' = -ω0² x - α x² - β x³
      with x(0 s) = x0, x'(0 s) = 0 m/s
      for t from 0 s to 10 s tolerance 1e-12
      until x' = 0 m/s
    return times(x)[end]

for x0 in [0.05 m, 0.10 m, 0.20 m, 0.40 m]
    T_ode = 2 half_period(x0)
    solve U(xm) = U(x0) for xm from -2 x0 to -0.01 x0
    T_exact = 2 ∫ 1 / √(2 (U(x0) - U(x))) dx from xm to x0
    ω = 2π / T_exact
    print "x0 =", x0, " T_ode =", T_ode to 11 digits, " T_exact =", T_exact to 11 digits, " x_minus =", xm to 10 digits, " coefficient =", (ω - ω0) / x0² to 8 digits
